-----------------------------------------------------
- User Defined Classes & Attributes (Introduction) - 
-----------------------------------------------------

At this point we have a class named Bunny, that is a blueprint (or template) for Bunny objects that will know nothing 
and do nothing.

		class Bunny {
		}

Perhaps our bunnies should know their name and their age. Let's add a couple of attributes to our Bunny class, so that 
a bunny has an age and a bunny has a name. We can visualize this revised Bunny class as a box with two compartments - 
one for the class name (Bunny), and one for the attributes (age, and name). This kind of visualization is called a Class 
Diagram, and it is a useful abstraction.

		Bunny
		age
		name

We add attributes (things that a bunny will know) to a class in Java by declaring variables inside the class. We can 
add an int variable for a Bunny to store its age, and a String variable for the Bunny to store its name.

		class Bunny {
   		   int age;
		   String name;
		}

Now, when we instantiate a Bunny object, it will know its age, and its name.
	
		Bunny myBunny = new Bunny();


-----------------
- Dot Notation - 
-----------------

We can access the attributes of an object in Java by using dot notation. This simply means that we use the variable name 
followed by a dot (a period), followed by the attribute name. For example, we can access the age attribute of our Bunny 
object like this.

		myBunny.age

Since this attribute is actually an int variable, we can interact with it just like it is any other int variable. 
For example, we can store an integer value in this Bunny object's age variable like this.

		myBunny.age = 3;

And we can likewise store a String value in this Bunny object's name attribute variable.

		myBunny.name = "Flopsy";

We can also easily retrieve the values stored in this object's attribute variables.

		System.out.println(myBunny.name + " is " + myBunny.age);

*** Code is in the Main.java to run to see examplation working ***

Classes allow us to define objects that are packages of related data (i.e. the name and age of a Bunny are related to 
that Bunny). Rather than managing a program as a disconnected collection of variables, we can group those variables 
into related sets by packaging the related sets in objects. All of the data related to a bunny can be packaged in a 
Bunny object, and all of the data related to a bank account can be packaged in a BankAccount object, and so on. The state 
of each object is contained in its attributes (which are just variables). This is an example of a powerful feature of 
object oriented programming called encapsulation.

Objects - encapsulate state.

